      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Using community-level metrics for credit prediction is indeed a promising strategy to reduce data lag, though regulators like the EEOC and ECOA require you to ensure these variables do not inadvertently lead to discrimination based on race or ethnicity. Metrics focused on objective stability, such as neighborhood workforce retention rates or changes in local property incidence, generally face fewer legal hurdles than subjective \"cohesion\" scores. However, you must carefully normalize any demographic data to prevent disparate impact while genuinely testing if these environmental factors have a statistically significant causal link to payment behavior in your specific dataset.",
